          body: `This is the most important thing to know about teething. The NHS says teething may cause a slightly raised temperature, but below 38C. A temperature of 38C or more is a fever and is not explained by teething. The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) also states that teething does not cause fever or diarrhoea, and the NHS says that although some people think teething causes diarrhoea, there is no evidence to support this.\n\nWhy does it matter? Teething overlaps with the age when babies catch their first colds, ear infections and tummy bugs. If every symptom is put down to teeth, a real illness can be missed. If your baby has a fever, diarrhoea, vomiting, a runny nose with a cough, or simply seems unwell, look for another cause and get medical advice rather than waiting for a tooth.`,
