          body: `Tooth decay can start as soon as a tooth appears, so brushing should start with the first tooth. Brush at least twice a day, with one brush just before bed (NHS). Advice on fluoride toothpaste and the first dental visit differs slightly between countries, so follow the guidance where you live.\n\nIn the UK, the NHS recommends a tiny smear of toothpaste for babies and toddlers up to 3 years old, then a pea-sized amount for children aged 3 to 6. There is no need to rinse with water afterwards, as this washes away the fluoride. NHS dental treatment for children is free, but not all dentists take on new NHS patients, and the NHS suggests taking your child along to your own appointments so they get used to the idea.\n\nIn the US, the ADA advises a smear of fluoride toothpaste no bigger than a grain of rice for children under 3, and a first dental visit within 6 months of the first tooth appearing, and no later than the first birthday. Advice in Germany, France, Brazil and Turkey differs in some details, such as fluoride strength and the timing of the first check-up, so parents there should follow their national recommendations and their own dentist.`,
